\begin{proof}
Choose distinct points $x,y\in X$. Define $d_x:X\to\mathbb{R}$ by $d_x(z)=d(x,z)$. This map is continuous because the metric $d:X\times X\to\mathbb{R}$ is continuous. Since $X$ is connected, $d_x(X)$ is a connected subset of $\mathbb{R}$. It contains $0=d_x(x)$ and $\delta=d_x(y)>0$, so it contains the interval $[0,\delta]$. Thus $d_x(X)$ is uncountable. Since $d_x$ maps $X$ onto $d_x(X)$, $X$ must be uncountable.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Because $X$ has more than one point, there exist $x,y\in X$ with $x\ne y$. Let $f:X\to\mathbb{R}$ be defined by $f(z)=d(x,z)$. The metric is continuous, hence $f$ is continuous. Therefore $f(X)$ is connected, as the continuous image of the connected space $X$. Moreover, $0=f(x)\in f(X)$ and $\delta=f(y)=d(x,y)>0$ lies in $f(X)$. A connected subset of $\mathbb{R}$ containing $0$ and $\delta$ contains $[0,\delta]$, which is uncountable. Since $f(X)$ is the image of $X$, it cannot have larger cardinality than $X$. Consequently, $X$ is uncountable.
\end{proof}
